## 5. Technology stack & trade-offs

### 5.1 Backend: ASP.NET Core vs all-TypeScript

|              | Next.js + ASP.NET Core (preferred)                                                                            | Next.js full-stack (TS only)                                       |
| :----------- | :------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | :----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Speed to MVP | Slower: two languages, two deployables, type sharing through OpenAPI codegen                                  | Faster: one deploy, shared Zod schemas for API and LLM output      |
| AI tooling   | Microsoft.Extensions.AI (IChatClient, structured output) is mature, and every provider has a .NET SDK or REST | Vercel AI SDK is excellent for provider-agnostic structured output |
| Domain rigor | Strong: NodaTime for dates, EF Core migrations, xUnit, Testcontainers                                         | Good: Temporal/date-fns-tz, Drizzle/Prisma                         |
| Long-term    | Clear backend boundary, good for growth and native apps later                                                 | Can drift into logic spread across Server Actions                  |

**Recommendation: keep Next.js + ASP.NET Core.** It is the stated preference, and the product's core value lies in domain logic: date resolution, the policy engine, and idempotent apply. C#, NodaTime, and EF Core handle that well. The two-stack cost is contained by three rules:

- Next.js is a **thin client**. It has no DB access and no Server Actions with business logic.
- Both are served **same-origin** behind one reverse proxy (`/` → Next, `/api` → .NET). That removes CORS and keeps cookie auth simple.
- TypeScript types are **generated from OpenAPI**, never hand-written.

If C# is not a strength, or the MVP deadline is very tight, switch to all-TypeScript. The architecture below maps one-to-one. This is decision D-01.

